{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_4r3ycuwi7s4p3waiowqwd6z4r2x25n74z5h3v3qf7e4lihadaeiq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 12 tests pass. Let me summarize:\n\nI read the C source files in `c_src/src/c_fsm.h` and `c_src/src/c_fsm.c` (which implement a finite state machine context with init, transition, process, and event functions) and the existing C test suite in `c_src/tests/test_c_fsm.c`. I built and ran the C test program with Unity to confirm ground truth behavior — all 4 C tests passed.\n\nThen I wrote `src/bin/test_cfsm.rs` with 12 tests covering the single `cfsm` module's public API:\n\n- `test_cfsm_version` — verifies the three version constants (0.3.0)\n- `test_cfsm_new_initializes_all_none` — verifies `CfsmCtx::new()` produces all-None fields\n- `test_cfsm_init_should_clear_handler` — mirrors the C \"clear handler\" test, including instance data update\n- `test_cfsm_init_with_none_instance_data` — edge case: passing `None` instance data\n- `test_cfsm_init_is_safe_to_use` — `process` and `event` must be no-ops when no handlers set\n- `test_cfsm_transition_should_set_enter_handler_only` — enter func with no handler-setting leaves all None\n- `test_cfsm_transition_with_none_clears_handlers` — passing None calls leave then clears handlers\n- `test_cfsm_process_calls_handler` — process delegated to handler 10x, mirrors C behavior\n- `test_cfsm_event_calls_handler` — event delegated to handler 10x, mirrors C behavior\n- `test_cfsm_transition_a_b_a` — complete A→B→A state transitions verifying handler addresses and enter/leave call counts\n- `test_cfsm_event_passes_event_id` — event ids (including negative) pass through unchanged\n- `test_cfsm_process_no_handler_does_not_crash` — silent no-op when no on_process handler\n\nAll tests pass. The Rust translation matches C behavior. (Note: the only behavioral difference from the C test suite is the version — C source has `CFSM_VER_MINOR=3` matching Rust's 3, even though the C test file accidentally asserts 2; I followed the actual source behavior, not the buggy C test assertion.)"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1185,"cache_read_input_tokens":37805,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1185,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e95d7131-c694-49b4-b57d-705e373016ea","uuid":"796efb87-b87a-4aad-a688-f0a3ef9ba4c7"}
